WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.410
Speaker 1: Please welcome
Swyx, Sarah Guo, Elad Gil,

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.650
and Chairman and Chief
Executive Officer

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.967
of Microsoft, Satya Nadella.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.600
Satya Nadella:
Hello, Sarah.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.670
Sarah Guo:
Hello.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.780
I'm so excited to be here.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.900
Welcome to a crossover
episode of "No Priors"

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.360
and "Latent Space"
with Satya Nadella.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.730
Congratulations on
an amazing Build.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.020
Satya Nadella: Thank
you so much, and it's great

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:31.920
to be with both of you.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.610
I listen to both of the
podcasts all the time.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.600
It's great to be on it.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.440
Elad Gil:
Thank you so much.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.010
Sarah Guo:
You were just talking

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.530
about these amazing
announcements

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.080
from across the Microsoft
estate all morning for,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.430
I think, three hours.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.150
What is the most important
reflection or takeaway you have?

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.820
Satya Nadella:
I'd say, there are --

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.710
perhaps, the biggest one
for me is, let's sort

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.570
of conceptualize this more as
an ecosystem play, as opposed

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.570
to a single model, or even
a single platform, right?

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.860
I mean, at least for me,
having grown up at Microsoft,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.550
having seen, whatever, four
major platform shifts,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:17.570
I sort of fall into that camp
where a platform is defined by,

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.090
fundamentally, its ability
to create more value

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.600
above the platform, versus
what's captured in the platform.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.360
If you view what's
happening right now,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.830
I think this morning's keynote
was, how can any company,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.210
whether it's an
AI-native company

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.760
or a traditional
enterprise company,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.201
participate as a first-class

00:01:38.213 --> 00:01:41.280
participant, where
they can point

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.830
to AI they create, right?

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.020
It's not that they don't
use other people's AI.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.440
Of course they will, but
to me, what's the path?

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.640
What's the recipe?

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.450
How do I do it?

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.770
What does the stack look like?

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.240
What does the tooling look like?

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.410
What is valuable?

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.190
How do you do that?

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:56.760
That's it.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.470
That's sort of our job to do.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.350
Sarah Guo: Ecosystem
strategy is very complicated,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.690
because you end up building
certain components,

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.910
partnering for certain
components, and supporting them.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.520
You just announced this
big suite of models.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.450
Tell us a little bit about the
training strategy for Microsoft.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.180
Satya Nadella: Yeah, so the
thing that we wanted to do

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.270
with the MAI models
was to build,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.790
and as Mustafa talked
about, first of all,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.350
a great lineage starting
with pre-training,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:33.390
with very good data quality,
doing all the ablations,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.780
making sure, because in some
sense, it's becoming even harder

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.750
to build a clean lineage
model, just because there's

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.980
so much stuff out there that
you truly need to ablate

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.890
out to be able to have a
fantastic pre-trained model.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.540
In fact, that's one of
the challenges of a lot

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.170
of the open-weight models is,
they look great on one benchmark

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.080
or two, but they're
not great on practice.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.830
That's why, in fact, even
our FDEs are pretty --

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.430
I've gotten really excited
about these MAI models,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:07.710
because how the heck can a
small 5B model hill climb?

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.980
It goes back a little bit to
what I think is, ultimately,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.410
the key thing to
do, which is try

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.590
to pursue finding
that cognitive core.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.000
To me, starting with a clean
lineage, then creating

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.800
that ability for companies
to be able to use this,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.390
not just as a generalist, but
to create their own specialist

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.840
by building this hill-
climbing scaffold around it.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.290
It's not just the model, but if
you have a hill-climb scaffold

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:41.070
around it, then you will
start building your RLE.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.840
You will start
collecting the traces.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.610
Most importantly, you'll
have private evals,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.700
because we know all the evals
out there are good, interesting,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.910
but they're not really that
critical at this point

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.530
because they all can be maxed.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.580
The point is, each company will
have its own private eval.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:02.130
That end-to-end platform story
around our models is, sort

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.290
of, what I think is interesting.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.180
Then the one other thing, Sarah,
since you brought that up,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.420
is, I do feel there's
a new frontier.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.740
People talk about
"the frontier,"

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.470
and are you "operating
at the frontier?"

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:17.510
Interestingly enough, if you
add a little temporality to it,

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.060
you can use, let's say, in fact,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.460
the Land O'Lakes demo we
showed was pretty cool.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.710
We used whatever,
GPT-5.5, right?

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.780
Then you collected
a bunch of traces,

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:31.410
and then you took a 5B reasoning
model and achieved higher.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.450
That is another aspect
of what it means

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.570
to operate at the frontier.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.730
Swyx:
Yeah, I think, first of all,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.800
I have to congratulate
you on, basically,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.240
on building a frontier
MAI lab inside

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.150
of Microsoft in two years.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.030
I'm wondering, you have
all this AI strategy

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.080
that you're rolling out.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.280
I'm wondering, what
do you know now

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.550
that you wish you could tell
yourself two or three years ago,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:56.100
three years for the Jensen
partnership, two years for MAI?

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.620
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I
mean, I think the thing

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.290
that I reflect quite a bit,
right, which is sort of,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.270
obviously, I got into all
this when I got excited

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.460
by the Scaling Laws paper,

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.370
and when even the OpenAI
partnership came about.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.540
When those folks said, hey,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.040
we're going to really
throw a lot of compute

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.920
at transformers,
and they've helped.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.140
The thing that I always
look back and say, wow,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.360
these things do have capability
that they're climbing up.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.350
I mean, a crude way of saying
it is, "intelligence is log

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.300
of compute," kind of works.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.860
Now, what I think we
underestimated, perhaps,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.300
is the real-world complexity
of deploying these

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.480
so that they actually
deliver the value

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:40.560
in the real world, right?

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.130
The outcomes, as measured
by any benchmark,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:49.950
are interesting/important, but
the true eval is when people

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:54.390
out there are able to do unique
things that they only can value,

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.300
and it's very measurable.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.570
That, I wish, we had sort
of even like had more

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.240
in our consciousness
as an industry.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.720
Right now, I think when people
say, "Wow, I don't want

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.570
to token max," it's an artifact

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.290
of us not having taught
ourselves as an industry

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.790
that we are using tokens

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.880
to create value every
step of the way.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.490
I think that's kind of what
I wish we had gotten there,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.720
but I'm glad we are here.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.670
Elad Gil: What are some of the
use cases that you've seen

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.010
that have created the most
value for your customers?

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:28.140
I know that people
talk a lot about code

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.130
and I think it's pretty clear

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.440
that that's something that's
having very large scale impact.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.390
Are there other areas
that you find in common

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.620
that your customers
are really benefiting?

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.600
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I think
to your point, obviously,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.440
coding is now god, but it's
interesting, by the way, Elad,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.870
to even talk about the coding.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.710
Coding has worked so well that
we now have to rebuild the IDE.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.130
I mean, it's kind of nuts to
see what we launched, and it's,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.650
like, oh, my God, I have
these hundred-agent sessions.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.770
The cognitive load that
transfers back to me

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.780
as a human is so excessive
that now I need a new UI.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.720
Oh, by the way, the chat

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.360
as the only artifact
was also impossible.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.680
That's why we need a canvas.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.170
It's kind of interesting
for all the things

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.650
about where is software
needed or where is UI needed?

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.120
You kind of need that, even for
code, in a fully agentic world.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.790
That said, one of the things
that we are starting to see --

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.650
we started seeing it with
CoWork, but even some

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:27.630
of the work we showed with
Autopilot, on what you see

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.300
with Claude, is a good one,
because if you sort of think

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.210
about it, a lot of human capital
is doing the glue work, right?

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:41.580
If you now can augment
that with tokens/agents

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:46.350
that are long-running,
durable, then your ability

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:49.470
to scale, even what
is still judgment

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:53.460
and glue work, gets
amplified like coding does.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.490
So you can -- I'm positive
that six months from now,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:00.420
we'll all be saying, oh,
wow, all through the night,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.570
there was a bunch of stuff
that all these autopilots

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.580
that I have working
on my behalf,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.550
with my delegated
authority, so to speak --

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:12.360
I can sort of give it, even, my
identity, did a bunch of work.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.060
Then, of course, I'll
need my ADE to say, well,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.540
what did you do, did I
do this work, and so on?

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.410
I think that that's where
compressing of workflows,

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.530
completing of tasks,
that's where I think a lot

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.460
of the value gets created.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:27.840
Elad Gil: I think you raised
a really interesting point,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.360
which is, there's the actual
agent doing the code

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.580
and then there's a
harness around it.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:33.930
That's the environment.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:34.830
That's the context.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.360
That's everything you're
setting up as a developer

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.100
around actually a coding agent.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.750
What is the harness
for the enterprise?

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:43.470
Is there an equivalent concept
for broader productivity work,

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.090
or how do you think
about that concept --

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:45.930
Satya Nadella:
That's right.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.720
In some sense, you kind
of want the harness

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:55.290
to define the models,
the data, and the tools,

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.290
so that you have a loop
across those three.

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.720
What we are trying to, first
of all, make sure is each

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.170
of our products that we build,
whether it's GitHub Copilot

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.470
or the Security Copilot, the
stuff we showed with MDASH,

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:10.230
or even the Discovery for
Science, it doesn't matter.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:15.810
All of them are multi-model
harnesses with tools access,

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:18.420
so that you can do this
progressive disclosure

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.360
of tools, even, so that
they're token efficient.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.641
Then you're feeding it with very

00:09:23.653 --> 00:09:26.160
rich context,
because that's sort

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.230
of the other hard
lesson we have learned

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.480
in the last two years
is, oh, my God.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:37.500
The amount of work you need to
do to prep the context layer,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.060
such that your plan can execute
in the most efficient way,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.920
is where the magic is.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.800
In our case, we have GitHub
harness, which, essentially,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.330
we're using across
all our products.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.700
It's available in Foundry
and we're open --

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.130
you can use your Llama
harness, whatever.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:58.500
You can use any open harness or
any harness of yours and train

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.190
with your tools, and multiple
models, and your context.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:05.820
That's the pitch, because right
now, a lot of dialogue is, hey,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.130
if I train the
harness, plus tools,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.950
and the model together,
you get evals.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.810
What we are proving out is --

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.590
and the best example of that is
what we did with MDASH, right,

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:22.140
because when it launched, it
found bugs, or vulnerabilities,

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.730
that were not found by Mythos.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.620
There is existence
proof, I would claim,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.180
that you can have a multi-model
harness that can, in fact,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.470
be more performant
in the real world.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:40.680
Sarah Guo: So, the premise
behind the training

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:44.250
at the independent frontier
labs is really, you know,

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.020
we're going to
have these models,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.490
and we'll have an API business.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:48.900
We'll support enterprises
and startups,

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.000
but a first-party product,
be it productivity, or code,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.860
or search, drives the
majority of revenue.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.510
That's a different value
equation than you're describing,

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.090
I think, with the
Microsoft ecosystem.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:02.370
If that's the case --
tell me if it's the case,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.230
because obviously you
have first-party products

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.570
and you have
enablement products --

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.010
what is the role of
the developer --

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.110
what's going to be hard
and the set of skills

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.360
and the value capture the
developer has in that world?

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.250
Satya Nadella: Yeah, so I think
that there's always going

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:18.630
to be the case that someone
who is super-successful

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:22.530
as a platform builder can also
have first-party products.

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:23.880
It was true with Windows.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:28.740
It is true with the SaaS side,
and the Cloud side as well,

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.380
with us, and others, and so on.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:35.220
The thing that is, it
should not be a limiter

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:37.830
to other people achieving
that same success, right?

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.590
That, I think, is
the core difference.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:44.820
The network effects this time
around, around intelligence

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.200
and such, because they learn
from data and not really lots

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.950
of data, it's just a few
samples that you have to see

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.610
to understand what's
novel about something.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:00.360
That's why the game
becomes how to protect.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.560
That's why I would say every
company, having private evals,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.600
may be the biggest IP.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:07.350
I think about it.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.910
What's that private eval

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.940
that you can then use, even a
frontier model to hill-climb on,

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.830
and not leak the traces.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:18.540
Maybe one of the biggest drivers
of IP -- so, in other words,

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:23.040
another asset test is, you
have an eval that's private.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:27.300
You're using Model
A. Can you switch it

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:31.200
to Model B and climb up?

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.510
If you can, then
you're in control.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.610
If you can't, you're
not in control,

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.620
and that's where even the
harness decision becomes

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:38.700
super-important, right?

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.830
Therefore, having
an open harness,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:45.540
letting all the models come in,
having your evals, your context,

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:49.860
your tools help you hill-climb,
I think, are the skills

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.520
that an AI-native startup
needs, a SaaS company needs,

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.430
or every enterprise needs.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.980
Swyx: Yeah, I think, in a
very real way, you are --

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.560
Microsoft, historically, was
an operating systems company

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.270
and then became a cloud company.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:05.490
Maybe the third act is
that you're a harness

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:10.980
or evals company, whatever
the conglomerate of concepts

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:12.510
that you want to put together.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.800
I think enabling every company
to have frontier intelligence,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:21.360
or I forget the exact term that
you use, is the mission.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:21.810
Satya Nadella:
That's it.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.080
Swyx: That is the platform
promise, that you build with us,

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.660
you will get your
intelligence for your data.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:28.530
Satya Nadella:
That's it.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.920
To me, that is the -- if
there was one tagline

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.010
for this entire developer
conference, it is,

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:38.610
"Can everybody operate
at the frontier

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.770
with their frontier
intelligence?"

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:44.040
To me, that is so important,
because otherwise,

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.590
I don't know how you
achieve stable equilibrium.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:50.610
Which is, how do I then go and
say, well, my company is going

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:54.060
to have a terminal value,
because I now know how

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.970
to continuously compound on top

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:59.130
of what's a platform
that gets better?

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:05.430
When Windows came out, Adobe
build, Autodesk build,

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:09.960
or even like take what Jensen
said, we built DX and he built,

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.720
you know, CUDA on top of it.

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:14.250
I mean, I always say
to Jensen, "God,

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:15.840
I got the short end of that.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.030
I wish we had recognized it."

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:23.160
Nevertheless, that idea that
you can build a platform layer

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.400
that someone else
can then extend out

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.500
and build their own intelligence
layer, in this case,

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:29.880
I think is everything.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.890
Without it, why have a
developer conference?

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:35.070
I can just come and have
you all just worship

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.840
at the altar of one model.

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:38.880
That's not a
developer conference.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.450
Swyx: Backstage, we had a
discussion about what is IP

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.040
or what is the
value in a company?

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.460
It used to be the length of
human experience at the company.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:51.960
Now it's this other thing, which
is the evals, the experience

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:53.970
in sort of applying
agents to the company.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:55.530
I just want you to
flesh it out a bit more

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:56.510
because it's very insightful.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:58.050
Satya Nadella: Yeah, it's
a great way to frame it.

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.880
At the end of the day,
every company is going

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:02.850
to have both the human
capital, that is still going

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:07.350
to be super-valuable, because
humans and their ability

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:12.120
to find the gaps that exist
at all times is going

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.460
to be the way we all
will create value.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:17.190
I'm definitely in the camp
that this is going to be

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:21.840
about expressing new forms of
human agency and ambition,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.450
even as token capital
goes up, right?

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:27.750
Let's say any corporation
has lots of tokens

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:29.430
and a lot of human capital.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.400
The question is, how do
you compound the two?

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:35.790
If you have -- if you take
in Teams, I have a bunch

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.340
of agents doing work and a
bunch of humans doing work

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:43.500
and the traces between those,
that is really important context

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:46.590
of how that enterprise
is creating value.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:50.790
Then, that goes back to train
not a generalist model,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:54.810
but to train the
company veteran agent.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:57.060
That is super-valuable again,

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.100
which is when a company
says it should, in fact,

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:00.600
go on to the balance sheet.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.190
That's how I think about it.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:06.330
In fact, maybe human capital
was never possible to put

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:09.090
on a balance sheet, because
you didn't know how

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:10.890
to capture the tacit knowledge.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.110
Whereas now, I think
you can with the agents

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:18.000
that have learned through
time, through all the traces.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.130
That's what, at least,
we think will happen.

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:21.300
Swyx: I think the
SEC is going to have

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:25.780
to have accounting standards
for token expertise.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:30.240
Sarah Guo: You're talking
about the equilibrium state

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:31.500
and a stable equilibrium

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.720
where companies have
this compounding value

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:35.610
and can see terminal
value for themselves.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:40.140
Another challenge to the
considered equilibrium of, okay,

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:44.490
there are applications and
workflows that are common

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.070
to a vertical or horizontal.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.200
This was the generation
of SaaS companies,

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.320
and Microsoft has lots of
SaaS properties as well.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.730
Then, there are things
that are very specific

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:56.760
to every enterprise that
they're differentiated against.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:58.920
I'm sure you have heard much
and participated in much

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:01.080
of the debate about
the end of software,

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.740
because all these workflows
are cheap to generate now.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:06.360
Do you think the
equilibrium looks different

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.750
between what agents get
built in enterprises,

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.000
versus in their
vendors in the future?

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:12.270
Satya Nadella:
Yeah,

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:14.192
so I think what's happening

00:17:14.204 --> 00:17:16.710
there is, we had
a particular way

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:23.150
we captured, I would
say, workflow in apps,

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.530
because we built a data model.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:29.280
We schematized some part
of some business process.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.710
We then built a bunch
of business logic,

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.260
and then we put a bunch
of UI on top of it.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.970
That's kind of what
every SaaS company --

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:36.870
Sarah Guo:
And a little configuration.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:38.190
Swyx:
For, like, 20 years.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.560
Satya Nadella:
Right, and that was it.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:44.100
Interestingly enough, now you
kind of get to relitigate

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.990
that vertical stacking.

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:49.230
I still think, for
example, that data model

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.700
that you built underneath every
SaaS application is super-good.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.320
Why reinvent it?

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:57.420
My general ledger better
be a general ledger.

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:00.420
I don't need new
schema creation.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:03.960
In fact, that entity
relationship is actually a

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:07.110
pretty good, robust thing
that I want to feed.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.130
Sarah Guo:
And you want it to be stable.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:08.670
Satya Nadella:
That's right.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:08.910
Sarah Guo:
Yeah.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:11.280
Satya Nadella: The same thing
with business logic, right?

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:14.940
If you look at -- we have
this product called Power BI.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:18.810
It is dashboards
galore people created.

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:19.980
The beauty underneath

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.580
that dashboard is a very
rich semantic model.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.270
Someone took the pain
to create a dashboard

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:30.480
and do all the measures,
and you want that.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:31.920
That's business logic.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:34.890
I want that to be
available to me.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.390
I think the challenge

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:40.440
of the SaaS business model
is, we packaged one way.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:43.710
We now have to learn how
to unbundle these things,

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:47.280
and rebundle in new ways, and
discover new business models.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.250
I mean, if you look at it,
what's happening today

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.130
with Microsoft 365
is a great example.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.950
We have this thing
called Work IQ.

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:58.460
In fact, what we are
realizing is, oh, my God,

00:18:58.460 --> 00:18:59.600
if you look at it, in fact,

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:01.550
there's a historical
parallel it.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:05.870
We sold first Exchange
and SharePoint.

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:08.180
Before Teams, we had a
thing called Lync Server,

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:09.120
and what have you.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.000
We thought, oh, that's all
going to move to the cloud,

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:14.760
but little did we realize
that the number of people

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:18.330
who will use servers in the
cloud is 10x, 100x, right,

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.770
because people were
not buying servers.

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.870
They were just buying
a subscription.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:26.640
The same thing is now happening
with M365, because with Work IQ,

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.040
we have exposed
what is, perhaps,

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:32.430
the most important database in
a company that never got used

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:36.480
as a database because it is
only captive to our apps.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:38.250
It was all -- email
operated on it.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.220
Teams operated on it, Word,
Excel, PowerPoint, SharePoint.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:45.090
Now, this is one of the coolest
things I get to do with Work IQ.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:49.070
I go to a GitHub repo, and I
say, hey, I attended a bunch

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:52.610
of design meetings last
week related to this repo.

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.230
Can you capture all that

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:56.000
and tell me what
changes I should make?

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:57.500
I mean, think about that.

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:00.440
It literally can go look
at all those transcripts,

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:03.680
come back with a plan
to change a code base.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:07.430
Previously, you could never
have thought of using M365

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:08.840
for something like that.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:12.960
The value creation opportunity
now in the agent world is,

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:16.350
in fact, 10x more, but it
does require us to have --

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.530
for example, there's going
to be usage around M365,

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.190
which is going to be perhaps
more than even the end users,

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:24.840
and we have to
even re-architect.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.580
In fact, what I use
to serve an inbox

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:34.080
or a mailbox cannot be
used to serve an agent.

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:35.580
That's sort of what we're doing.

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.520
Sarah Guo: I don't believe
in permanent business models

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:40.980
for any of these domains,
but in the near term,

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:45.780
do you have a prediction
between outcomes-based pricing,

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:49.380
token-based pricing,
enterprise bundles?

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:50.520
Satya Nadella:
Yeah, the way I think

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:53.460
about this is,
always we've had --

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:56.040
let's even take the
per-user pricing.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:21:00.300
The per-user pricing
is really an artifact

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:04.200
of someone creating a budget
needing certainty.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.090
It's the most important thing.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.090
If somebody wants a budget,
they need a per-user,

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:13.920
and per-user is just a set
of entitlements to usage.

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:15.270
That's kind of what it is.

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:19.260
The way is, the first bundling
will be, take some usage,

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:23.550
bundle it into per-user stacks,
and then sell subscriptions.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.680
Subscriptions, I think,
are going to be there.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:27.150
Per-user is going to be there.

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.610
Then, the next big thing
will be consumption.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.740
People will say, "I
want consumption."

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:34.350
It's also possible that people
will say, "I don't even want

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:35.820
to pay for any of
the subscriptions

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:37.440
or the consumption outcome."

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:40.320
Remember, most
people love outcomes

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:41.580
until they have an outcome,

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:42.840
because once you
have an outcome,

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.120
it's like giving away a royalty.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:48.570
I've talked to customers who
love outcome-based pricing

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.630
and I say, "I'm all in,"
until they -- oh, my God.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:52.620
What are you talking about?

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:53.940
You're sharing in my outcome?

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:54.240
No, no, no.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:56.610
I want you to go back
to per-user pricing,

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:58.410
and I want to do
consumption pricing.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:02.250
I think that debate will go on.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.680
All of these business models
have a particular time

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:08.280
and a place, versus
one to rule them all.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.320
If anything, if
you're a SaaS vendor

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.410
or you're a platform vendor,
having that flexibility --

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.930
and quite frankly, we
faced this with GitHub.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:19.350
We just recently announced a
per-user pricing on GitHub

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:24.240
because little -- GitHub
Copilot was constructed

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:29.370
at a per-user level before
we understood the intensity

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:31.170
of usage of agents.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.750
It was an interactive
way for a developer

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:36.390
to use code-complete
maybe tasks.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:40.020
It was not, oh, I
launched 10,000 agents

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:42.990
that are going on all day.

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:45.240
That is what the
adjustment is about.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:48.480
Now that we really want -- there
will always be a per user,

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:51.090
but they will have to
be a consumption meter.

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:52.290
Elad Gil: How do you
think about the durability

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:53.250
of SaaS more generally?

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:56.910
One thing I've observed is, in a
lot of enterprises internally,

00:22:56.910 --> 00:22:59.520
there will be teams that
almost have agent euphoria.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.290
They're so excited
about the explosion

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:02.550
of things they can build
that they're trying

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:03.870
to rebuild a lot
of applications.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.190
They're going to their
SaaS vendors and saying,

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:06.810
"We're not going to work
with you anymore," or,

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:08.760
"We're considering an
internal project."

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:09.960
It seems like in
six to nine months,

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.970
maybe some of those people will
come back and say, actually,

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:13.860
we can't rebuild everything.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:14.970
How do you think
about what's durable

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.550
in this world and what isn't?

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:18.180
Satya Nadella:
I think we have to go

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:27.180
through one full budget cycle on
this to really see the emergence

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.700
of the equilibrium, because
at the end of the day,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:33.450
there's marginal cost to
even generating the app.

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:34.770
In fact, there can be --

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:39.360
a simple way to say it is, you
should always acquire something

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:44.220
if the marginal cost of building
and maintaining something

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.530
on your own is higher.

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:51.180
That should be -- it's
a quantifiable thing.

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.000
The maintenance
part is important.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.060
You've got to remember,
hey, all the security stuff

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.540
that now AI will find, you
better fix them, too, fast.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.550
Of course, there's a coding
agent to help you with it,

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:06.060
but then that burns tokens, so
whose responsibility is it?

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:09.990
It's kind of like a cycle that
you've got to think through.

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:12.000
I think we have gone
through the excitement

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.400
that I can generate
a lot of software.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.370
I think the next thing would be,
what software do I really want

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:18.420
to generate?

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.240
What software do I want
to use from others?

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:24.840
How do I compose these two
into some agentic workflow

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:27.330
that I have agency over?

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:29.790
I think there'll be very
little tolerance for anybody

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:35.310
who is inflexible at the vendor
level, but at the same time,

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:39.420
I think that anyone who has
got that flexibility shows up,

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.450
delivers the value,
will be back again.

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:45.540
We're selling software, but with
just different business models,

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.340
in fact.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.260
Swyx: Speaking
about building software,

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:51.390
one of my favorite moments from,
I think, a previous Build,

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:54.480
maybe one or two years ago,
was they had a section

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.010
of you building
your own software.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:57.810
I'm curious if you're
building anything now.

00:24:57.810 --> 00:25:01.140
Satya Nadella:
Yeah, so I think, first of all,

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:04.830
let's face it, building
software has made it possible

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:08.670
for even the incompetence of
a CEO of a company like ours,

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.710
you can build, so thank God.

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:19.050
That said, I do feel that
something like GitHub Copilot

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:23.430
to me, and especially the new
sessions app, or the new app,

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:29.550
has just made it so much more
possible for you to have agency

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.940
over artifacts that you felt
you couldn't touch before.

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:37.740
For me, as a CEO, even to go to
a code base, to be able to learn

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:41.400
about it -- I remember joining
Microsoft long back, first.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.590
Then, you said, man, everybody
had to go in and look at,

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:45.750
you know, whether it's Cutler's,
Malik, or what have you,

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:50.130
to learn how to
do good C++ code.

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:54.930
So now, that ability to be
more full stack up and down is

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:57.780
so good, but that
doesn't mean every one

00:25:57.780 --> 00:25:59.580
of us should be doing
the same thing.

00:25:59.580 --> 00:26:02.010
The question is, how do
you then have the ability

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:06.090
to inspect things, learn
things, see things?

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:07.590
I think it's just so much more.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.000
To me, what I'm building a lot

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:13.320
of is these long-running
Foundry agents.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:14.910
There's Autopilots.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:19.410
The easiest thing is, to me -- I
think I just built one last week

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:23.970
where the idea was, hey,
can I have an agent

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.850
that is continuously monitoring?

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:30.720
Essentially, it's my own
Chief of Staff Autopilot.

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:33.810
We're going to have that,
obviously, in Scout.

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:38.250
That's what we showed, but it is
so easy and trivial to build.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:39.630
I took Work IQ.

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.310
I said, "Take Work IQ.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:45.270
Go and build a Foundry
long-running agent.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:49.920
Store all the memory
using Rayfin," basically

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:51.660
at my back end as a service.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:54.810
Lo and behold, it built it, and
not only built it, I could say,

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.040
"Publish to Teams,"

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.140
and it published the
damn thing to Teams.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:03.450
The ability to have
some end-to-end project

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.670
like this complete is
just pretty miraculous.

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:07.800
Elad Gil: How do you think that
impacts the different types

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.710
of engineering roles that
exist in the future?

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:13.080
Right now, I think there's
a dozen different types

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:16.950
of engineers that you can be
from QA, front end, et cetera.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.270
There's a big swath.

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:20.460
I've heard some people argue
that in four or five years,

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.860
we'll basically end up with
four engineering roles.

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:25.050
It'll be people who
are managing agents.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.540
It'll be forward-deployed
engineers, or FDEs.

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:30.240
It'll be security engineers,
and then people working

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.390
on large-scale infrastructure
for a small number of services.

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:34.860
Then everything
else just collapses

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:35.970
into the agentic world.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:37.740
Do you think that's a
correct view of the world?

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:39.330
Satya Nadella:
Yeah, I think we'll have

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:41.970
to experiment our
way through it.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:45.750
What you said is what -- there
are some very at-scale things.

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:50.670
At LinkedIn, they did
structurally change

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:53.130
and basically built up
a new discipline called

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:54.480
"full-stack builder."

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:58.020
They went and said, hey, let's
bring people from design

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:00.870
and product management,
front-end engineering all --

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:04.140
put them together, but
also have an edge.

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:07.110
It's not like the design person
still doesn't have the design

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.420
edge, or the front-end person
doesn't have the front-end edge,

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:12.960
but you can give yourself
a bigger scope in roles

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:15.870
so that you're not
confined to one role.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:20.460
Then, equally, infrastructure
has become very critical.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:24.750
In other words, I mean, RLEs,
one thing we've realized is even

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:29.820
for the Excel team, for
example, building the RLE,

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:33.330
in which a reward can be
learned, is actually one

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:36.510
of the hardest
infrastructure problems.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:38.370
You kind of need
even new talent,

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:39.570
distributed systems people,

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:43.200
even in what was considered
an end-user app team,

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:44.550
because it's a
different skill set.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:48.420
So, yes, Infrastructure Science
is the other one, obviously.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:51.630
I think we'll see
how these evolve.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:55.830
I mean, always, the world will
have a bunch of specialists.

00:28:55.830 --> 00:29:01.920
I think the generalist role is
going to be the most exciting,

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:06.840
because the leverage of a
generalist is where we are going

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:08.730
to see the maximum returns.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:10.830
When you said,
"Hey, are you coding?"

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.390
I'm now a generalist.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:16.650
I basically translated
knowledge work,

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:22.830
which I did, where I created a
Word document, or a spreadsheet,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.690
and now I can build an app.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:30.090
It's in the same sentence,
that idea that, oh, wow,

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:33.450
my generalist skills have gotten
higher-leverage, I think,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:35.400
is what we're going to
see across the board.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:38.100
Sarah Guo: Music to the
ears of CEOs and VCs

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:39.810
that are a little
dangerous and a lot of --

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:41.220
Swyx: It's a golden
age for idea people.

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.560
Sarah Guo: Idea people,
yeah, with a lot of agency.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:45.330
If you take that idea
of personal agency

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:50.310
and you just zoom it out to
the organizational context,

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:53.010
my partner, Mike Vernal, who
actually started his career

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:55.650
at Microsoft, just wrote
an essay where one

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:57.180
of the big takeaways
is, it's an age

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:59.730
where you can be much more
ambitious, and you need

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:03.060
to be, given the pace of the
environment and how quickly,

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:04.680
actually, users and
companies are open

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.900
to adopting new technologies.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:08.160
How do you think about --

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:10.170
I feel silly asking this

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:12.810
of somebody running a
trillion-dollar-plus company

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:13.740
already, but how do you think

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:16.860
about how Microsoft can
be more ambitious now?

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.360
Satya Nadella:
It's a great question.

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:25.470
I think the thing in these
types of transitions is

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:35.250
to have a conceptual model
of how work can change to go

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:40.110
after outcomes that you could
hardly imagine previously.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:43.080
In fact, Kevin Scott
has this nice line,

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:47.010
which is, when you can
make the impossible --

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:50.790
when you're making hard things
easier, that's sort of one point

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:53.820
of leverage, but
true ambition is

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:57.630
about making the
impossible possible.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:01.260
Now, the thing that is
missing a little bit in all

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:03.090
of our organizations is, what is

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:06.960
that new conceptual model
of what can we build?

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:10.050
What was impossible
and what can we build?

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:11.550
I'll give you one
example of this,

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:14.700
which is, I take great
inspiration from the people

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:18.600
who are managing
the Azure network.

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:21.210
This is from last year.

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:21.930
We were scaling.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:24.630
You saw that I talked
about how we built

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:28.500
in the last 15 months more
Azure capacity than we built

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:29.940
in the first 15 years.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:30.630
I mean, it's crazy.

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:31.350
Sarah Guo:
Wild, yeah.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:32.220
Satya Nadella:
It's pretty wild.

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:33.570
It's the same team.

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:36.210
They saw that and they said,
"This just ain't going to work

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:39.390
if we don't
reconceptualize our work."

00:31:39.390 --> 00:31:42.690
They built -- essentially,
they said, our job is not

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:44.250
to do Azure networking.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:47.190
Our job is to build
the agentic system

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:48.810
that does Azure networking.

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:54.420
These are the folks managing
the 500-plus fiber operators,

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:56.580
managing the van all over.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:59.760
Fiber operations, ultimately,
is a physical operation.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:00.870
Things get caught.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.420
Things have to be repaired.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.700
We have fancy words
called DevOps, and so on.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:08.640
Basically, emails are coming in,
and you've got to go respond

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:10.290
to them, take care of it.

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:11.850
They've built this
agentic system.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:13.230
They even have a
character for it.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:17.070
It's called Miles, and it
sort of does all this stuff.

00:32:17.070 --> 00:32:21.390
They started sort of screaming
for more tokens, and so on.

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:23.040
They were saying, look, we
don't need a headcount.

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:27.060
We need tokens in order to be
able to manage our operation.

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:32.010
That reconceptualization
of what their work is --

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:35.370
they basically took their
work and made it meta.

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:38.610
That meta work is
now their new work.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:41.550
In the '80s, if somebody
had come to us and said,

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:43.500
4 billion people are going
to get up in the morning

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:47.310
and start typing, my model would
have been, we need four billion

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:49.890
typists? But we're
not doing typing,

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.420
we're doing knowledge work.

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:54.150
That, to me, I think is it.

00:32:54.150 --> 00:32:56.640
Whether it's Microsoft, or
whether it's any organization,

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:00.420
is to give ourselves
permission to do new types

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:04.980
of metacognition, meta
work, using these new tools

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:08.010
to change the
outputs that matter

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.200
and then, really make the
impossible possible.

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:14.340
Completing that dot, or the
connective tissue across those,

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:15.840
I think, is where a lot

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:18.240
of the enterprise value
will get created.

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:19.890
Swyx: Can we talk
about data centers?

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:20.700
Elad Gil:
Yeah, please ask.

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:21.480
Swyx:
Oh, okay.

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:26.790
Well, this leads nicely
into the data center part.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:28.830
I'm just impressed with the
sheer scale of the buildout

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:31.110
from Microsoft, but
also everyone else,

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:34.740
that this is redefining what
it means to be a hyperscaler.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:39.840
I just feel like that is an
unprecedented scale on finances,

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.820
on the way you run the
company, but also,

00:33:41.820 --> 00:33:45.000
the communities
that are impacted.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:46.650
Just talk a bit more about what
you're seeing on the ground,

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:47.520
like when you visit your --

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:49.860
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I think
there are two aspects of it.

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:54.930
Obviously, the buildout
is extraordinary.

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:58.590
Nothing like this has happened,
and it's great to be one

00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:01.920
of the participants in it, but
you brought up the other part.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.910
I think at this point,
it's clear that unless we,

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:12.420
as an industry, are very
principled about ensuring

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:15.090
that the benefits of all
the stuff we're talking

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:19.710
about are felt in real ways
at the community level --

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:23.930
because this is not
just a campaign.

00:34:23.930 --> 00:34:27.110
It has to be real, where
people are saying, look,

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:30.020
this is not changing the
prices on energy for me.

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:33.480
In fact, if anything, it's
bringing down prices because,

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:35.580
long-term, there's going
to be a better grid.

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:37.500
There is going to
be more energy.

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:40.410
Water consumption is,
in fact, not sort of --

00:34:40.410 --> 00:34:42.330
in fact, water is
being replenished.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:47.100
You've got to really educate
folks on truly what's happening,

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:49.740
the closed-loop systems
we are building.

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:51.240
We have to invest
in the training,

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:52.680
the jobs, the tax base.

00:34:52.680 --> 00:34:56.100
In fact, the least-talked about
stuff is the amount of jobs

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:58.650
that get created
during construction,

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:01.620
after construction, what's
the tax base that's there

00:35:01.620 --> 00:35:02.790
in the community?

00:35:02.790 --> 00:35:06.300
All this has to be real.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.670
If that is the case, then
we will have permission.

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.410
If it is not, we won't
have permission.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:11.520
It's as simple as that.

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:14.160
Which is we -- I think
we have to take it

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.620
as an industry pretty seriously.

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:19.410
I think it's good for
communities to be skeptical,

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:24.090
ask the hard questions, for us
to do the hard work, earn that,

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:28.800
but at the end of the day,
if we can really be --

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:32.190
I've always felt in human
history, if you use a lot

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:36.570
of energy, but also create
a lot of value for society,

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:38.670
the story has been fantastic.

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:41.190
If you don't do that,
it's not been that great.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:44.100
This time around, I'm a firm
believer that, ultimately,

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:47.640
if you do have a token economy
that drives productivity,

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:49.590
that drives economic growth,

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:53.880
that drives widespread
participation,

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:56.130
better health outcomes,

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:59.370
then, I think we will
be in a great place.

00:35:59.370 --> 00:36:02.150
That's at least what we
all have to be focused on.

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:04.430
Swyx: Yeah, it makes
me think, actually,

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:05.960
that with all these
initiatives that you're doing,

00:36:05.960 --> 00:36:07.670
it might be easier to see ROI

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:09.740
in the communities first
before an enterprise.

00:36:09.740 --> 00:36:11.990
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I
mean, I think both sides.

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:13.520
In fact, it comes back together.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:16.710
It has to be, the people in
the communities are going

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.210
to be employed, are going

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:21.540
to be participants
in the real economy.

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:23.340
That's, I think, the question.

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:26.220
If the broad economy
is doing well

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:30.750
and the communities are doing
well, the dots get connected.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:34.770
The market forces are such
that we will connect the dots.

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:35.640
That, I think, is it.

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.050
You ought to be able
to see the evidence.

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:39.510
It can't be about
any one company,

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:42.180
but it has to be
broad economic growth

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:45.340
and broad community permission.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:50.640
Elad Gil: What have you most
updated in your personal models

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:53.700
on -- regarding the
societal impact of AI?

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:55.980
Satya Nadella: So you're
saying what's the --

00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:57.240
Elad Gil: What have
you updated most on,

00:36:57.240 --> 00:36:59.160
in terms of societal
impact of AI?

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:06.090
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I think
the most critical thing is the

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:09.240
first question we even started
with, which is, we need

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:14.310
to tell the story
and make it real

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:20.280
that everybody has a
real shot to participate

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:24.150
as a first-class participant
in this new economy.

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:29.640
That's kind of, I think, in
the next 12 months, 18 months,

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:33.420
we need a way for people
to say, oh, wow, I get it.

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:37.200
There's going to be tremendous
capability, a tremendous amount

00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:41.640
of infrastructure, but I can
see what is going to happen.

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:45.510
Whether it's the benefits, like
health outcomes, or my ability

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.060
to create a startup,
or my ability

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:52.920
to run my local store
more efficiently,

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:56.940
it's just happening, and I
see that benefit myself.

00:37:56.940 --> 00:38:00.950
That, to me, you know,
earning that permission

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:03.200
in a path-dependent
way, we can't wait.

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:07.310
The one thing I've learned is,
I think the world is going

00:38:07.310 --> 00:38:09.470
to be very skeptical of tech

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:13.440
and tech companies
that say, "Trust us.

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:14.580
We've got it.

00:38:14.580 --> 00:38:17.580
The future is going
to be glorious."

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:24.510
You kind of have to
deliver tangible benefits,

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:28.470
and quite frankly,
politicians winning elections

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.890
because they have advocated
for that, that will be

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:33.960
at least my adjustment.

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:37.410
Without it, thinking
that somehow --

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.000
because it's too important
this time around.

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:42.900
It's too much of the economy
for it not to be the case.

00:38:42.900 --> 00:38:44.850
Sarah Guo: One very
simple framework I have

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:49.140
for what is going to
be the broad benefit

00:38:49.140 --> 00:38:52.350
of AI, beyond the
communities just working

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:55.680
in technology, are sort
of wealth creation.

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:57.630
It's going to happen in a
ton of different companies,

00:38:57.630 --> 00:38:59.160
startups and large companies.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:00.480
Then you have healthcare.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:01.980
You had amazing demos today.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:03.180
There are companies
like OpenEvidence.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:05.430
I think that is happening.

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:08.970
Education seems like another
one that's an obvious good,

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.520
where we haven't seen as
much impact as I'd expect.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:14.490
Do you have a hypothesis on why
that might be, or if it'll come?

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:16.230
Satya Nadella: Yeah, I mean,
I think this is where, again,

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:18.690
how we think about
education, how --

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:22.560
recently, I met with the
founders of Alpha School

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:25.380
and learned a lot about
what they were going about,

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:27.960
and it's fascinating
to listen to how

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:31.770
to even rethink, what does
education really look like,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.200
because I think it's
actually very important.

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:36.540
I'm not saying anything
traditionally being done is

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:37.380
less important.

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:40.440
I was even looking at the
-- it's fascinating to see.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:42.743
I forget the which
Stanford class

00:39:42.755 --> 00:39:44.730
it was, the Agent Guidelines

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:49.800
for CS something, because you
still need people to learn.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:51.480
I think it's interesting,
in an AI class,

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:55.200
that people were learning how to
apply softmax more appropriately

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:58.590
versus saying, "Hey,
fix my training run."

00:39:58.590 --> 00:40:01.350
I think learning
concepts is important.

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:02.940
It's going to be critical,

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:06.090
but the way we create
the incentives,

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:09.900
what are the credentials, how
we value those credentials,

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:11.700
what is the employment
opportunity

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:15.510
for those credentials. I think
that there is a complete change

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:20.310
that has to happen, given the
way to get to information,

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:22.740
the way to educate yourself,
the way to continue

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:25.260
to keep yourself updated
has changed so much.

00:40:25.260 --> 00:40:30.330
I think, interestingly enough,
maybe the next big startup

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.180
and success story
could be someone

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:39.540
who builds a new university,
or a new pedagogy, of how

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:42.540
to get someone to go
through a curriculum

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:45.120
and find economic opportunity.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:46.380
That's highly valuable.

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:48.330
Sarah Guo:
Well, that has felt, perhaps,

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:50.820
impossible for a long time, but
it's a great note to end on,

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:52.530
something that
might be possible.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:53.130
Thank you, Satya.

00:40:53.130 --> 00:40:53.730
Satya Nadella:
Thank you so much.

00:40:53.730 --> 00:40:54.030
Thank you.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:54.750
I appreciate it.

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:56.520
Thank you all.

00:40:56.520 --> 00:41:03.500
[MUSIC]

